1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,500
How Long to Keep Employee Records in Singapore. A 2026 guide for Singapore employers and HR teams

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:17,158
Under Singapore's Employment Act, employers must keep employee and salary records covering the latest two years for current employees.

3
00:00:17,158 --> 00:00:23,620
For ex-employees, the last two years of records must be kept for one year after the employee leaves.

4
00:00:23,620 --> 00:00:29,500
Payroll and tax records sit under longer rules: IRAS and ACRA both set a five-year minimum.

5
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:36,787
Record: Employee and salary records, current employees. Minimum retention: Latest 2 years. Source: Employment Act, MOM. Record: Employee and salary records, ex-employees.

6
00:00:36,787 --> 00:00:41,817
Minimum retention: Last 2 years, kept 1 year after leaving. Source: Employment Act, MOM. Record: Payroll and other records used for tax.

7
00:00:41,817 --> 00:00:46,883
Minimum retention: 5 years from the Year of Assessment. Source: Income Tax Act, IRAS. Record: Accounting records, including salary costs.

8
00:00:46,883 --> 00:00:51,617
Minimum retention: 5 years from end of financial year. Source: Companies Act s199, ACRA. Record: Personal data no longer needed.

9
00:00:51,617 --> 00:00:55,500
Minimum retention: No fixed period: dispose once no purpose or legal need. Source: PDPA 2012, section 25.

10
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:01,719
Since 1 April 2016, employers must keep detailed records for all employees covered by the Employment Act.

11
00:01:01,719 --> 00:01:08,279
Employee records: such as address, NRIC or work pass number, date of birth, start and leaving dates, working hours, and leave taken.

12
00:01:08,279 --> 00:01:12,255
Salary records: pay, allowances, deductions and overtime for each salary period.

13
00:01:12,255 --> 00:01:18,319
MOM accepts soft or hard copy, so scanned records can meet the requirement if complete, legible and producible on request.

14
00:01:18,319 --> 00:01:21,500
Source: Ministry of Manpower (MOM), as summarised in the article

15
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:31,922
1. List record types: contracts, salary, leave, appraisals, medical, work pass. 2. Assign the longest minimum: usually five years for payroll and tax. 3.

16
00:01:31,922 --> 00:01:40,113
Set a trigger: date of leaving, or end of financial year. 4. Choose the format: scan active files; keep paper only where required. 5.

17
00:01:40,113 --> 00:01:43,500
Dispose securely: certified destruction, and record it.

18
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:52,670
Does the PDPA set a retention period? No. It requires you to stop retaining personal data once it is no longer needed for its purpose or for legal or business reasons.

19
00:01:52,670 --> 00:01:58,884
Archived and scanned files still count as retained. Can employee records be kept digitally? Yes. MOM accepts soft or hard copy.

20
00:01:58,884 --> 00:02:04,314
Scans should be complete, legible, indexed and retrievable on request. What happens after the retention period?

21
00:02:04,314 --> 00:02:09,500
Paper files are shredded with a certificate of destruction; digital files are deleted with an audit trail.

22
